<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karakalpaks</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Karakalpak people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karakalpaks are a Kipchak Turkic people living mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> delta south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>, in the Republic of Karakalpakstan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Their name means black hats. Long subject to the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>, they received their own autonomous region in 1925, and the drying of the Aral Sea since the 1960s has gravely damaged their homeland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karakalpaks.jpg" alt="Karakalpaks" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Karakalpakstan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means black kalpak, or black hat. Many accounts link the Karakalpaks with the Cherniye Klobuki, a Turkic confederation of the 11th century whose name also means black hat in Russian and who were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>; but there is no archaeological or historical evidence for a connection. The Karakalpaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> are regarded as subgroups of the same Uzbek Confederation, which arose in the 15th century after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> collapsed.</p><p>Documents from the reign of Abdullah Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> (1583–1598) mention Karakalpaks near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sighnaq">Sighnaq</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. Recent archaeological evidence suggests that they formed as a confederation of tribes in the late 15th or the 16th century on the Syr Darya or its Zhany Darya outlet, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> of the Lesser Horde, which would explain why their language and customs resemble those of the Kazakhs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The 18th century was the most difficult time in their political development. Many Kazakh clans became subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>, and many Karakalpaks fled to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. In that century they settled along the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and in its delta on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. Since 1810 their fate has been closely tied to that of the Khiva Khanate, and under its rule the poor were hired as farm labourers by the rich and by officials. The annexation of the region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> did not end exploitation, according to the literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>About 900,000 Karakalpaks live worldwide, and more than 770,000 of them in Karakalpakstan in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukus">Nukus</a> is the capital. They are not the largest group in their homeland, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> increasingly outnumber them. Their language belongs to the Kipchak–Nogai group of Turkic languages, with Kazakh and Nogai, and is written in Cyrillic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters; before the Soviet period it was rarely written, in a modified Perso-Arabic script. Karakalpaks are mainly followers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, and dervish orders such as the Kubrawiya are common.</p><p>The oral tradition includes legends of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and Dede Korkut, and of fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayaz-kala">Ayaz-Kala</a>. The 19th-century poets Ajiniyaz and Berdakh wrote of the hard life of the people and their dissatisfaction with the feudal order. The drying of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> has destroyed the local fishing industry and much of the grazing and farmland in the north of the delta.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karakalpaks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Hungary the title of Comes Bissenorum, Count of the Pechenegs, lasted for centuries, and some historians believe that Pechenegs contributed to the origins of the <b>Karakalpaks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Karakalpaks</b>, a Kipchak Turkic people living in the Amu Darya delta under the Khanate of Khiva, received their own Karakalpak Autonomous Oblast in 1925, created from lands of the Khorezm People's Soviet Republic and attached to the Kazakh ASSR.</blockquote><a href="/state/karakalpakstan">Karakalpakstan</a></li></ul></section>
